  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nA 1024 mb high is currently centered over Michigan with troughing \nwell to the north and weak northwesterly flow moving in from the \nupper ridge out west. The aforementioned surface high will move to \noff of the Mid-Atlantic coast early tomorrow. New surface high \npressure will shift down from the Upper Plains the latter part of \nthe week, keeping precipitation at bay for now.\n\nTemperatures will remain above normal with a gradual warming trend \neach day. When we round out the work week mid to upper 80s are \nforecast from the central to southern valley. The coolest morning of \nthe week will be this morning where the upper half of our forecast \narea will see lows in the 40s; 50s for lows for the remainder. \nThough the air is on the drier side, fog near bodies of water is \nstill possible.\n\nOur next chance of precipitation will arrive sometime this weekend. \nThere are uncertainties as to what and when. As of last night, a \ntropical disturbance with a 80 percent chance of development \ndetermined by the NHC, will drift towards the central northern Gulf. \nDeterministic models have great differences in what they think will \noccur. If one thing's certain is that our area will see rain lifting \nup from the south Saturday and possibly into Monday. Some gusty \nwinds, most likely from a strong pressure gradient, could be \npossible and of course will be monitoring.",
